Noida Apartments: Comparing Configurations Before You Shortlist
The number of bedrooms is often the first thing people notice when comparing noida apartments, but it should not be the only factor guiding the shortlist. A 2 BHK, a 2 BHK with a study and a 3 BHK can serve very different household needs even when their overall areas appear relatively close.
The more useful comparison is the floor plan itself. Room proportions, carpet area, storage, balconies, kitchen placement and the way different parts of the home connect can have a direct effect on how comfortable the apartment feels after it is furnished.
What Should You Compare in Noida Apartments?

Start with the floor plan rather than the brochure headline.
The first comparison should be between the actual spaces available for everyday use. Check the bedrooms, living and dining area, kitchen, bathrooms, balconies and circulation space. This gives a clearer picture of the apartment than looking at the total sale area alone.
Carpet area is particularly useful when comparing layouts because it indicates the area within the apartment that can be used by the occupants. Sale area and built-up area are different measurements, so figures from different categories should not be compared as though they represent the same thing.




Room Size Matters More Than the BHK Label
Two homes carrying the same BHK label may still have different proportions.
Look at the bedroom dimensions and consider the furniture that needs to fit inside. A bedroom should have enough space for the bed, wardrobe and necessary movement without making the room feel congested.
The same applies to the living room. Mark out the likely position of a sofa, television unit and dining table before deciding that a particular layout works. A floor plan that looks spacious on paper can feel different once furniture is placed.
Check How the Rooms Connect
The relationship between rooms is another useful comparison point.
A well-organised layout should make movement through the home straightforward. The kitchen should have practical access to the dining area, bedrooms should have appropriate privacy and bathrooms should be conveniently positioned.
Balconies also deserve attention. Check whether they connect to the living room, a bedroom or another part of the home and consider how that arrangement fits your routine.
Should You Choose a 2 BHK, 2 BHK + Study or 3 BHK?
The answer depends on how the household intends to use the additional space.
A 2 BHK provides two bedrooms and can be suitable for households that do not require a separate study, guest room or additional private space. Its appeal often lies in keeping the layout focused on the essential rooms.
A 2 BHK + study adds flexibility. The additional room can be used as a home office, study area, reading room or occasional guest space. Its usefulness depends heavily on its size, location and access within the floor plan.
A 3 BHK adds another full bedroom. This can provide greater separation between family members, guests and work-related activities. It can also allow one room to remain flexible rather than requiring an existing bedroom to perform multiple functions.
The important point is that a larger configuration should solve a real space requirement. Extra rooms are useful when they match the way the household lives.
How Should You Read the Area Figures?
Area figures can become confusing when different properties use different terminology.
For a proper comparison, note whether the figure refers to sale area, built-up area or RERA carpet area. Then compare the same measurement across the shortlisted apartments.
For instance, CRC Sublimis currently lists a 2 BHK with a total sale area of 982 sq. ft. and RERA carpet area of 603 sq. ft. Its 2 BHK + study configuration is listed at 1,170 sq. ft. sale area and 727 sq. ft. RERA carpet area.
Those figures demonstrate why simply comparing the headline area can be misleading. The more useful exercise is to examine what the additional space actually provides.
The same principle applies when comparing 3 BHK layouts. CRC Sublimis lists different 3 BHK configurations, including a 1,305 sq. ft. option and a 1,485 sq. ft. option, with different bathroom arrangements and carpet areas.
Look at Storage Before You Finalise
Storage is easy to overlook during the first property visit.
Consider where wardrobes, kitchen cabinets, appliances, utility items and other household belongings will go. A floor plan may have sufficient overall area but still offer limited practical storage.
Ask for the detailed floor plan and identify spaces that can accommodate wardrobes or additional cabinets without interfering with movement.
What Should You Check During a Site Visit?
Once a floor plan makes the shortlist, the physical apartment should confirm what the drawing suggests.
Start with natural light. Visit the bedrooms, living area and kitchen rather than judging the apartment only from the entrance. Window placement, surrounding buildings and the apartment's orientation can all affect the experience of the space.
Next, check ventilation and room proportions. Stand inside each bedroom and consider whether the furniture arrangement shown on the plan is realistic.
Electrical points, door openings, bathroom access and balcony dimensions are also worth checking. These details become much more important after possession because they affect furniture placement and daily routines.

Does a Study Make a Meaningful Difference?
A study can be valuable when the household needs a dedicated space without moving to a larger bedroom count.
For someone working from home, it may provide separation between professional and personal activities. For a family with school-going children, it can become a dedicated study area. It may also work as a reading room or flexible space.
However, the label alone should not determine the decision. Check the actual dimensions, natural light, ventilation and access. The usefulness of the room comes from its layout, not simply from the word “study” on the floor plan.
How Can You Build a More Practical Shortlist?
A useful shortlist should contain only apartments whose layouts work for the household.
Begin by deciding the minimum number of bedrooms required. Then identify whether an additional study or flexible room would genuinely be useful. After that, compare carpet area, room dimensions and storage rather than relying solely on total sale area.
